  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "👻 Ghost Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for hauntings, from the Grey Lady to a friendly little Wisp."
    },
    "intro": "Real ghost stories name their ghosts in a few simple ways: by colour (the Grey Lady, the Brown Lady), by who they were (the Drummer, the Monk), or by where they're seen. This **ghost name generator** makes names in those patterns, along with Irish banshees, restless <a href=\"victorian-name-generator.html\">Victorian</a> spirits and friendly ghosts for Halloween.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How real ghosts got their names",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>By colour:</strong> Grey Ladies are reported at castles and country houses all over Britain. The Brown Lady of Raynham Hall became famous after a photograph published in 1936.</li><li><strong>By who they were:</strong> drummers, monks and nuns appear in many local legends, usually tied to an old story about how they died.</li><li><strong>By place:</strong> Anne Boleyn is said to haunt the Tower of London, where she was executed in 1536.</li><li><strong>By what they do:</strong> the Headless Horseman of Washington Irving's <em>The Legend of Sleepy Hollow</em> (1820), or the poltergeist, German for \"noisy ghost\".</li></ul><p>Plenty of Britain's named ghosts haunt places where you can book a room, from coaching inns to castles. If you'd rather meet a ghost than name one, our sister site <a href=\"https://hauntedhotels.uk/?utm_source=thenamegenerators&amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;utm_campaign=ghost-names\">Haunted Hotels UK</a> lists more than 80 haunted hotels you can stay in, with the story behind each ghost and the rooms to ask for. Or, for an evening rather than a whole night, join a <a href=\"https://hauntedhotels.uk/ghost-tours?utm_source=thenamegenerators&amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;utm_campaign=ghost-tours\">ghost tour</a> in York or Edinburgh.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Banshees",
        "content": "<p>The banshee comes from Irish folklore: in Irish she is the <em>bean sí</em>, \"woman of the fairy mound\". She doesn't harm anyone. Her keening, a high mourning cry, warns a family that one of them is about to die.</p><ul><li><strong>Old families:</strong> tradition says she keens only for the oldest Irish families, and the names most often given are the O'Neills, O'Briens, O'Connors, O'Gradys and Kavanaghs.</li><li><strong>How she's seen:</strong> sometimes a young woman, sometimes an old one, often combing her long hair. In some tales she is a washer at the ford, washing the clothes of the one who will die.</li></ul><p>The <em>Banshee</em> style gives her a modern Irish first name with a keening epithet. The banshee is part of a living tradition, so treat her kindly.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Using the names",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>For a story or game,</strong> give the ghost a name the locals use (the Piper of Gallows Hill) and a real name someone has to find out.</li><li><strong>For Halloween,</strong> the friendly ghost names suit costumes, decorations and pets.</li><li><strong>For a haunted house,</strong> a colour and a figure is all it takes: every old house deserves a Grey Lady.</li></ul><p>For other things that go bump in the night, try the <a href=\"vampire-name-generator.html\">Vampire</a>, <a href=\"werewolf-name-generator.html\">Werewolf</a> and <a href=\"zombie-name-generator.html\">Zombie</a> name generators.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "Why are so many ghosts called the Grey Lady?",
        "answer": "Because witnesses describe what they see: a pale, grey figure in a long dress. The name stuck at many castles and houses across Britain."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does poltergeist mean?",
        "answer": "Noisy ghost, from German: poltern (to make noise) and Geist (ghost)."
      },
      {
        "question": "What's a good name for a friendly ghost?",
        "answer": "Something soft and short: Wisp, Boo, Flicker or Glimmer. Choose \"Friendly ghost\"."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is a banshee?",
        "answer": "A spirit from Irish folklore, the bean sí or \"woman of the fairy mound\", whose keening cry warns a family of a coming death. She's a mourner, not a monster."
      }
    ]
  }
